<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henryk Sienkiewicz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1846">1846</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henryk Sienkiewicz was a Polish novelist and winner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1905. He is best known for Quo Vadis and for his historical novels, among them the Trilogy set in seventeenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> (Krzyżacy, 1900), which culminates in the Battle of Grunwald and shaped Polish popular memory of the Order as an aggressor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henryk-sienkiewicz.jpg" alt="Henryk Sienkiewicz" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henryk_Sienkiewicz_ca_1885_(45871548)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henryk Sienkiewicz was born on 5 May 1846 at Wola Okrzejska in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. His family were impoverished Polish nobles, on his father's side descended from Lipka Tatars who had settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. He grew up on family estates and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, where he studied at the Imperial University and moved from medicine and law to the Institute of Philology and History. He finished in 1871 without a diploma, having failed the examination in Ancient Greek, and lived in poverty as a part-time tutor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He debuted as a journalist in 1869 and wrote under the pen name Litwos. In 1876 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> with the actress Helena Modrzejewska, and Gazeta Polska paid for his journey in exchange for travel essays, which won him a popular following. He stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1879. From 1883 he turned to historical novels. With Fire and Sword was serialised from 1883 to 1884, The Deluge from 1884 to 1886 and Sir Michael from 1887 to 1888. Together these make the Trilogy, which established him as the most popular contemporary Polish writer.</p><p>He wrote the first chapters of Quo Vadis in February 1895, and the novel was finished by March 1896 and soon gained international renown. He began serialising Krzyżacy, The Teutonic Knights, in 1897, and it was finished in 1900. In 1900 the Polish people gave him an estate at Oblęgorek near Kielce, where he later opened a school for children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> remained partitioned between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and Sienkiewicz used his fame to influence world opinion for the Polish cause. He criticised the policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanization</a> of the Polish minority in Germany, supported the Września schoolchildren in 1901, and in 1907 appealed to the intellectual community amid German attempts to expropriate Polish land. A moderate, he declined to stand for the Russian Duma and gave to struggling writers.</p><p>In 1905 he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature for his merits as an epic writer. In his acceptance speech he said that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> had been pronounced dead and that the prize was proof that she lived. He died on 15 November 1916.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henryk Sienkiewicz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Order's memory was later used in rival national mythologies: <b>Henryk Sienkiewicz</b>'s novel The Teutonic Knights cast the knights as aggressors, while German nationalists and later the Nazis claimed them as forerunners of eastward expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wola Okrzejska, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Vevey, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.75,22.13&amp;year=1891" aria-label="Wola Okrzejska on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5050 374 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henryk Sienkiewicz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5500 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5500 644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.75,22.13&amp;year=1891">What was here in 1891? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
